    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">982</span><span class="rf-text">The Hudud al-'Alam, the first document to name Lahore, describes a town with impressive temples, large markets and huge orchards</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1020s</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> conquers Lahore and makes it part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1241</span><span class="rf-text">The Mongol army sacks and ruins the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1584</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> begins re-fortifying the citadel and makes Lahore the Mughal capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1799</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> captures Lahore and makes it the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1849</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> is annexed to the British Indian Empire and Lahore becomes the capital of the province</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1940</span><span class="rf-text">The All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> passes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore-resolution">Lahore Resolution</a> demanding a separate homeland for the Muslims of India</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1947</span><span class="rf-text">Riots in the weeks of Partition end with Lahore awarded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> on 17 August</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xuanzang.jpg" alt="Xuanzang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xuanzang_w.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lahore lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> River in central-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. No definitive record of its early history exists, and its unclear background has given rise to several theories about its establishment. Oral tradition holds that the city was named after Lava, son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, who supposedly founded it, and the Lahore Fort has a vacant temple dedicated to Lava. The origin of the name is otherwise unclear. Early Muslim historians recorded it as Luhawar, Luhar and Rahwar, and one theory derives it from Ravawar, a name possibly drawn from the Ravi, known as the Iravati in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>. Another theory derives it from the word lohar, meaning blacksmith.</p><p>The historians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> mention no city near the site during his invasion in 326 BCE, which suggests that it had not been founded or was not noteworthy. Ptolemy mentions in his Geography a city called Labokla near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenab">Chenab</a> and Ravi, which may have been ancient Lahore or an abandoned predecessor of it. The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> described a large and prosperous unnamed city when he visited the region in 630 CE, and it may have been Lahore.</p><p>The first document to mention Lahore by name is the Hudud al-'Alam, written in 982. It describes a town with impressive temples, large markets and huge orchards. Before the Ghaznavid conquest in the 11th century, Lahore appears to have been the capital of Punjab under the maharaja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anandapala">Anandapala</a> of the Odi Shahi dynasty, who moved his capital there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udabhandapura">Udabhandapura</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-aibak"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-aibak.jpg" alt="Qutb ud-Din Aibak" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-aibak">Qutb ud-Din Aibak</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grave_of_Sultan_Qutb_al-Din_Aibak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Muhammad Umair Mirza, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the Ghaznavids Lahore became a cultural and academic centre renowned for poetry, and poets and scholars from other cities of the empire gathered there. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-aibak">Qutb ud-Din Aibak</a> it attracted poets and scholars from the medieval Muslim world, and it had more poets writing in Persian than any other city. Its greatest prosperity came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, and it has more Mughal monuments than either <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>. The Tughluq ruler Ghiyath al-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a> saw it flourish briefly between 1320 and 1325.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s time most residents lived in suburbs outside the walls, and only 9 of the 36 urban quarters, known as guzars, lay inside them. Akbar rebuilt the walls, extended them east of the Shah Alami bazaar, set up the Akbari Mandi grain market and established the Dharampura neighbourhood. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, born in Lahore in 1592, renovated the fort in white marble, built the Naulakha Pavilion in 1633 and laid out the Shalamar Gardens in 1641. The population of pre-modern Lahore probably reached its peak in his reign, with perhaps six times as many people in the suburbs as in the Walled City. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> built the Alamgiri Bund along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> in 1662, the Badshahi Mosque in 1673 and the Alamgiri Gate of the fort in 1674.</p><p>Under British rule the city grew south of the Walled City. Model Town was established as a garden suburb in 1921. An estimated 200,000 people lived in Lahore in 1901.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-din-mangburni"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jalal-al-din-mangburni.jpg" alt="Jalal al-Din Mangburni" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-din-mangburni">Jalal al-Din Mangburni</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jalal_al-Din_Khwarazmshah_confronts_Chinggis_Khan_across_the_Indus._Rashid_al-Din,_Jami%E2%80%98_al-tawarikh,_National_Library_of_Russia,_St_Petersburg,_MS._Dorn_289,_fols._125v%E2%80%93126r_(Jalal_al-Din_Khwarazmshah_with_weapons).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1529 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city was the capital of the Odi Shahi maharaja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anandapala">Anandapala</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Mahmud conquered it between 1020 and 1027 and made it part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a>. It became the eastern capital of the Ghaznavids in 1152 and their sole capital after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> in 1163. The Ghurid ruler Muhammad captured it after the siege of 1186, and after his assassination in 1206 it was the first capital of the Mamluk dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>'s sultans later preferred Delhi as a safer capital, and the city passed between the Delhi Sultanate, the Khwarazmian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-din-mangburni">Jalal al-Din Mangburni</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khokhars">Khokhars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, who took it in 1398.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> captured Lahore in 1525, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> reconquered it in February 1555 after the interval of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a>. From 1584 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> made it the Mughal capital. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s death in 1707 its governors held it for ever weaker emperors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> invaded in 1739, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> occupied it in 1748 and invaded again in 1751, 1752 and 1757. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> occupied it in 1758, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durranis</a> withdrew in 1765. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/misl">Bhangi Misl</a> captured it in 1767, and in 1780 it was divided among three rulers.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> took Lahore in 1799 and made it the administrative capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> seized it in February 1846, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> was annexed in 1849. It remained the capital of the province under British rule until 1947.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Lahore&#8221; cites where it covers Mediaeval era, British colonial period:</p><ul><li>William Glover, Making Lahore Modern, Constructing and Imagining a Colonial City (Univ. of Minnesota Press, 2008)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/akbar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akbar.jpg" alt="Akbar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Govardhan._Akbar_With_Lion_and_Calf_ca._1630,_Metmuseum_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Govardhan / Mir Ali Heravi, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lahore's Walled City was built piecemeal, with small cul-de-sacs, as katrahs and galis developed beside neighbouring buildings, and it had thirteen gates. Some neighbourhoods were named for a religious or ethnic community, but they were typically diverse and not dominated by it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> of the old city included the Sarafa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> and Brass, Kashmiri and Shah Alami bazaars. In the late Mughal period the city was closely tied to market towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasur">Kasur</a> and Eminabad and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> and Batala.</p><p>Several faiths left marks on the city. According to one account, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> was the oldest religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, and Jain temples stood at places still called Thari Bhabrian and Gali Bhabrian. Lahore's first church was built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> in the late 16th century and levelled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> in 1632. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> shrines, among them that of Ali al-Hujwiri at the Data Darbar and that of Mian Mir, hold annual festivals called urs. Lahore also contains some of the holiest sites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a>, including the Gurdwara Dera Sahib, built to mark the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a> Dev's death in 1606.</p><p>The city was a centre of Persian letters. In the 17th century the Lahore Quartet of Abu al-Barakat Munir Lahori, Muhammad Saleh Kamboh, Shaikh Inayat Allah Kamboh and Chandar Bhan Brahman, four local scholars and poets, promoted one another and revived Persian literacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The British laid out their Civil Station south of the Walled City, where British and locals mixed along The Mall, and the Cantonment, where laws against mixing existed.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Lahore&#8221; cites where it covers Religion, Old City, Architecture, Parks and gardens:</p><ul><li>William Glover, Making Lahore Modern, Constructing and Imagining a Colonial City (Univ. of Minnesota Press, 2008)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/babur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babur.jpg" alt="Babur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humayun_and_Babur_(Late_Shah_Jahan_Album)_Babur_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">c.1650, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lahore was devastated by the Ghaznavid invasion, and Malik Ayaz rebuilt and repopulated it. A Mongol army sacked and ruined it in 1241, and Mongol attacks followed in 1287, 1298 and 1305. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarmashirin">Tarmashirin</a> sacked it in 1329. By the time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> took it in 1398 he did not loot it, because it was no longer wealthy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> sacked it in 1525. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s death in 1707 the city declined: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s invasion in 1739 shifted trade routes away from it, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> ports that served it silted up, and it had nine different governors between 1745 and 1756. By the end of the 18th century its remaining people lived inside the walls while the suburbs lay abandoned.</p><p>The partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in 1947 brought the worst riots of Partition to Lahore, as the city's fate was fiercely contested. The 1941 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 671,659 people, 64.5 per cent of them Muslim and about 35 per cent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a>. Hindus and Sikhs disputed these figures before the Boundary Commission and argued that the city was only 54 per cent Muslim; two thirds of its shops and 80 per cent of its factories belonged to Hindus and Sikhs. Muslims, Hindus and Sikhs were all both victims and perpetrators. The riots of March and April 1947 destroyed 6,000 of Lahore's 82,000 homes, and violence rose through the summer despite British armoured personnel. By late August 66 per cent of Hindus and Sikhs had left, and the Shah Alami <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">Bazaar</a>, once a largely Hindu quarter, was burnt down. On 17 August Lahore was awarded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Lahore&#8221; cites where it covers Durrani invasions:</p><ul><li>William Glover, Making Lahore Modern, Constructing and Imagining a Colonial City (Univ. of Minnesota Press, 2008)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh.jpg" alt="Ranjit Singh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharaj_Ranjit_Singh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Colonel James Skinner (1778-1841), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Lahore Fort and the Shalimar Gardens are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>'s rule restored some of the city's lost standing but his armies plundered Mughal monuments for building materials and stripped the marble of the Tomb of Asif Khan, the Tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-jahan">Nur Jahan</a> and the Shalimar Gardens. The Badshahi Mosque was used as an ammunition depot and a stable. Ranjit Singh built the Hazuri Bagh Baradari in 1818 and a gurdwara marking Guru Ram Das's birthplace in 1801. In 1841 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-singh">Sher Singh</a>'s soldiers placed guns on the minarets of the Badshahi Mosque and destroyed the fort's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">Diwan</a>-e-Aam. The British built the Lahore Museum and the Mayo School of Industrial Arts in the Indo-Saracenic style around 1887, and Sir Ganga Ram, called the father of modern Lahore, designed many of its important buildings.</p><p>Lahore was central to the independence movements. Nehru moved the Declaration of Independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> at Bradlaugh Hall on 31 December 1929, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagat-singh">Bhagat Singh</a> was hanged in the city's jail in 1931, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore-resolution">Lahore Resolution</a> in 1940. After Partition its industrial production fell to one third of its earlier level and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, not Lahore, became the capital. Lahore was capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a> from 1955 until 1970, and the Minar-e-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> was completed in 1968 where the resolution was passed. The Walled City restoration began in 2009.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Lahore&#8221; cites where it covers Modern:</p><ul><li>Gyanesh Kudaisya, The Aftermath of Partition in South Asia (Routledge, 2004)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib<span>date unknown</span></a><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram<span>1876</span></a><a href="/songs/sare-jahan-se-accha">Sare Jahan se Accha<span>1904</span></a><a href="/songs/dama-dam-mast-qalandar">Dama Dam Mast Qalandar<span>1956</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Lahore, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> in Pakistani <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, was first named in 982 and served as a capital of the Ghaznavids, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>. Its Partition riots of 1947 preceded its award to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and it is now the capital of Punjab province.</em></p>

